{
  "format": 1,
  "about": "Shared vectors of the expression language. The Rust tests, the converter's preview script and the runtime run the same file. Each vector is an expression ('expr') or a text template ('tpl') and the expected outcome: a static type and a value, a rendered text, or an error code. See the 'contract' entries below.",
  "contract": {
    "types": "Flat map from a model path to its type. Struct types are written 'struct <path>', list elements use '[]' in the path, 'T?' is nullable, 'enum(a|b)' lists the values, 'key<kind>' is a list key. A root is any first segment of these paths; a struct type named 'struct p' has the field 'f' at 'p.f'.",
    "vars": "Loop variables: name -> { type, origin }. A variable is a root of its own: 'c.name' reads the model value at the path 'c.name'.",
    "state": "Skin state: path below 'state.' -> type. Values are read at 'state.<path>'.",
    "extra": "Whole paths outside the schema (a mod's own model): path -> type.",
    "model": "Flat map from a full path to its value. A path that is absent reads as null. JSON null, bool, number and string are those values (a number written without a fraction is an integer); an array is a list of that length; {\"$color\": \"#rrggbbaa\"} is a color; any other object is a record that is present. A vector may carry its own 'model' that replaces entries.",
    "host": "loc(key, a, b) renders as key|a|b with each argument written as an insert would write it (null is empty); integers are grouped by thousands with a comma.",
    "expectation": "'type' is the static type as the checker writes it (alternatives joined by '|', then '?' when nullable, 'null' for the literal null); 'value' is the result (a color as {\"$color\": ...}, a list as {\"$list\": n}, a record as {}); numbers are compared as numbers; 'warns' is the number of log lines (default 0); 'error' is the code of the first error, found when parsing or typing; 'text' is the rendered template.",
    "numbers": "Integers are exact below 2^53; a float prints with up to four decimals, halves rounded away from zero, trailing zeros dropped, no exponent; every operation without a result (division by zero, null operand) is null."
  },
